  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the pinned cylinder do?",
      "a": "The cylinder carries the tune. Each pin is set at the right place around it and along it, so it plucks the right comb tooth at the right moment as the cylinder turns.",
      "asks": [
        "How is the tune stored in a music box?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do music box notes sound different?",
      "a": "Each tooth of the steel comb is tuned to a note. Longer, heavier teeth vibrate more slowly and sound lower; shorter teeth vibrate faster and sound higher.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the steel comb make notes?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What keeps a music box playing at the same speed?",
      "a": "A small fan called a governor spins fast as the music plays. The faster it spins, the more air pushes against it, so it stops the spring from speeding the tune up.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the fan in a music box for?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What powers a music box?",
      "a": "A coiled steel mainspring. Winding the key tightens it, storing elastic energy that drives the gears as it slowly unwinds.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do you wind a music box?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who invented the music box?",
      "a": "The music box with a tuned steel comb was invented by the Swiss watchmaker Antoine Favre in Geneva in 1796.",
      "asks": [
        "When were music boxes invented?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Antoine Favre of Geneva invented the comb-type music box in 1796.",
    "Shorter comb teeth vibrate faster and make higher notes."
